course: dictionary
lesson: 745
title: Words for places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bathhouse is a building with baths where people go to wash, bathe and relax. Look at the word in two parts, bath and house, and you can build its meaning from the parts you already know. «Medieval people washed their hands and faces every day, and many towns had public bathhouses.» Public means for everyone to use, so a public bathhouse was a place many people could visit.
explain: Context gives more clues. The sentence says people washed every day and that towns had public bathhouses, so the word names a place for washing. Try your meaning in the sentence: «many towns had public bathhouses, which were places for people to wash» still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. A bathhouse can be a Roman building with hot, warm and cold rooms, a Turkish hammam, or a Japanese sento. An old meaning is a building at a beach where swimmers change, so always check the context to see which meaning fits.
fact: A bathhouse is a building with baths for public use, where people go to wash, bathe and relax.
fact: «Public» means for everyone to use.
fact: Roman bathhouses had hot, warm and cold rooms, called caldarium, tepidarium and frigidarium, and they were places to meet friends.
fact: Turkish baths (hammams), Japanese bathhouses (sento and onsen) and Finnish saunas are all kinds of bathhouses.
fact: In many cultures public bathhouses were needed when homes had no baths.
exercise: «Medieval people washed their hands and faces every day, and many towns had public bathhouses.» What is a bathhouse? | Bread baked in a hot oven | A tall tower where the king lived | *A building with baths where people go to wash, bathe and relax | A kind of boat for swimming
exercise: «Many towns had public bathhouses.» What does public mean here? | *For everyone to use | Covered in paint | Only for one family | Very old and broken
exercise: Which of these is a hot room in a Roman bathhouse? | Frigidarium | Stadium | *Caldarium
test: Which of these is a Japanese bathhouse? | *Sento | Sauna | Caldarium | Hammam
test: An old dictionary says a bathhouse can be «a building at a beach where swimmers change». What does this tell you? | A bathhouse is the same thing as a beach | *A word can have more than one meaning, so check the context | Bathhouses are always at beaches | Swimmers must never use a bathhouse
exam: A text says «the bathhouse was warm and steamy, and people chatted and relaxed there». What kind of place is it? | *A place to wash and relax | A cold, empty cave | A busy railway station | A school gym
